FM 2025.csv","added":true,"status":"ready","problem":null}],"answer":{"status":"done","text":"**Method.** I ran k-means on all 5,000 customers, using the three columns (recency, frequency, spend) standardised to z-scores. I tried k = 2 to 8 and kept the k with the highest mean silhouette score.\n\n**Choice of k.** k = 4 scored highest, with a mean silhouette of 0.841. The scores for the other values were:\n\n| k | Mean silhouette |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | 0.727 |\n| 3 | 0.788 |\n| **4** | **0.841** |\n| 5 | 0.654 |\n| 6 | 0.602 |\n| 7 | 0.571 |\n| 8 | 0.551 |\n\n**Segment profiles.** Segments are numbered S1 to S4 from highest to lowest mean spend. Recency is in days since last purchase, frequency is purchases in 12 months, and spend is spend in 12 months. The file doesn't state a currency.\n\n\n| S | n | rec | freq | spend |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 300 | 7.69 | 39.79 | 13,029.08 |\n| 2 | 700 | 30.27 | 19.9 | 9,505.35 |\n| 3 | 1,400 | 89.87 | 7.974 | 5,998.72 |\n| 4 | 2,600 | 250.28 | 2.01 | 601.4 |\n\n\nReading the profiles, S1 looks like very recent, very frequent, high-spend customers. S4 is the largest group and looks lapsed, with about 250 days since last purchase and about 2 purchases. S2 and S3 sit between them.\n\n**Stability.** I re-ran k = 4 with 5 different random seeds (0 to 4).\n- All five runs gave the same segment sizes: 300, 700, 1,400 and 2,600 customers for S1 to S4.\n- Every customer landed in the same segment in every run. The adjusted Rand index against the first run was 1.0 for each seed, so the segments are fully stable.\n- The silhouette score was the same, 0.841, in every run.","citations":[],"figures":[],"analyses":[{"number":1,"title":"RFM k-means segmentation","language":"python","code":"from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score, adjusted_rand_score\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\ndf=inputs[\"d\"].copy()\nprint(df.columns.tolist(), len(df))\ncols=df.columns[1:4]\nX=StandardScaler().fit_transform(df[cols])\nsc={}\nfor k in range(2,9):\n    l=KMeans(k,n_init=10,random_state=0).fit_predict(X)\n    sc[k]=silhouette_score(X,l)\nprint(sc)\nbest=max(sc,key=sc.get)\nlabs={}\nfor s in [0,1,2,3,4]:\n    labs[s]=KMeans(best,n_init=10,random_state=s).fit_predict(X)\ndef rank(l):\n    m=df.groupby(l)[cols[2]].mean().sort_values(ascending=False)\n    mp={c:i+1 for i,c in enumerate(m.index)}\n    return np.array([mp[x] for x in l])\nR={s:rank(l) for s,l in labs.items()}\ndf['S']=R[0]\ng=df.groupby('S').agg(n=(cols[0],'size'),rec=(cols[0],'mean'),freq=(cols[1],'mean'),spend=(cols[2],'mean'))\nprint(g)\nfor s in R: print(s, adjusted_rand_score(R[0],R[s]), (R[0]==R[s]).mean(), silhouette_score(X,R[s]))\nfor s in R: print(s, np.bincount(R[s])[1:])\nresult(\"best_k\",best,\"best k\",\"\")\nresult(\"prof\",g.reset_index(),\"profile\",\"\")","inputs":[{"kind":"range","name":"d","citation":{"passageId":"5a766c75-d801-493c-85cd-2bebe6e376bf","documentId":"541f6085-cdb6-43a1-9c80-5bb3f5e76150","documentName":"Customer RFM 2025","contentHash":"421e998cf8e749b8e06178fda4f1b11d1345baa8a54845bfeabb7eff0fa4d086","pageFrom":1,"pageTo":5,"location":{"kind":"rows","sheet":null,"from":1,"to":5001},"quote":"Customer\tDays since last purchase\tPurchases in 12 months\tSpend in 12 months","check":"found","checkReason":null},"range":"A1:D5001","rows":5000,"columns":4,"rowCount":5000,"cut":false,"sha256":"caa45ff9ddb040623b0c4a917170ea6bc671cbec01a1927a169e321c277621f8"}],"versions":{"language":"python","languageVersion":"3.14.2","runtime":"Pyodide 314.0.7","packages":{"contourpy":"1.3.3","cycler":"0.12.1","fonttools":"4.62.1","joblib":"1.5.3","kiwisolver":"1.5.0","matplotlib":"3.10.8","numpy":"2.4.6","packaging":"26.1","pandas":"3.0.2","patsy":"1.0.2","pillow":"12.2.0","pyparsing":"3.3.2","python-dateutil":"2.9.0.post0","pytz":"2026.1.post1","scikit-learn":"1.8.0","scipy":"1.18.0","six":"1.17.0","statsmodels":"0.14.6","threadpoolctl":"3.6.0"},"helper":"bf16406b48beea6811